---
§1 — Motivation
You write firmware for systems that have to work.
Not "work well enough." Work. A brake controller that fires in under a millisecond. An
actuator that closes when it's told to close. A safety interlock that cannot be talked out of its
decision by a clever input.
You know what a specification is. You know what a failure condition is. You know that "it
usually works" is not a sentence that survives a design review.
And you have been handed, in the last three years, a class of software that:
· produces a different answer to the same question on Tuesday than it did on Monday
· cannot be tested exhaustively
· cannot be certified against the standards you actually work under
· runs somewhere else, on someone else's hardware, at someone else's discretion
· consumes power at a scale your industry would never tolerate for any other component
You have been told this is the future, and that the standards you work under will be amended
to accommodate it.
What is missing is not a better model. It is a deterministic inference primitive that fits inside
the safety envelope you already work in.
Something with a fixed output for a fixed input. Something with a bounded execution time
you can put on a timing diagram. Something that fails closed. Something you can put on an
MCU next to the rest of your firmware and treat like any other function.
This document is a reference implementation of that missing primitive.
