§3 — ST-T1024 in one page
The full standard lives at:
https://github.com/actuing/ST-T1024-STANDARD-FOR-EMBEDDED-LLM
You do not need to read it to build this artifact. But you should know where this artifact sits
inside it.
The full architecture (what ST-T1024 eventually is)
· N independent domain tiles — knowledge shredded into isolated silos. Only one tile
powered on at a time.
· 75% ROM / 25% SRAM per tile — immutable facts in hardware-burned ROM; sovereign
updates in encrypted SRAM.
· Ternary arithmetic — values in {−1, 0, +1}. No floating-point. No multipliers in the hot path.
· FIS triage — a fuzzy rule set that routes an input to exactly one tile.
· FSM scheduling — a hardware-enforced cycle counter that guarantees a bounded
execution time.
· Tiny deterministic transformer per tile — renders the decision into human language. Not a
sampler.
· SK-1024 sovereign updates — cryptographically signed tile updates, gated by
national-authority keys.
The pipeline (what runs, in order)
```
input
→ harvest (normalize; deterministic; no allocation)
→ FIS triage (route to exactly one tile)
→ FSM schedule (bounded cycle counter; halts on overrun)
→ tile execute (ternary transformer; fixed function)
→ render (human-readable sentence; deterministic)
→ power down (0 V idle)
```
Every stage is a function. Same input, same output, always. The FSM counter covers the
whole chain. There is no sampler anywhere.
What this artifact implements
A single-tile, 32-command slice of the above, on a host and on an MCU. Enough to prove
the shape. Not the full standard.
Full ST-T1024 This artifact
1024 tiles 32 tiles
Ternary transformer per tile Ternary matcher + lookup render
ROM/SRAM split Compile-time constants
SK-1024 sovereign updates Not implemented
Certified to four norms Not certified
If this artifact works, the standard has its first verified instance. If it fails, the standard is
wrong and you will have found where.
