§5 — What we are testing
Three claims. Each is testable. Each has a fail condition.
Claim 1 — Determinism
Statement. For any input, the pipeline produces bit-identical output across every run.
Test. Feed 1,000,000 inputs. SHA-256 the full output stream. Run twice. Compare hashes.
Fail condition. Two different hashes. That would mean somewhere in the pipeline, there is a
hidden state, an uninitialized variable, a timing dependency, or a source of entropy. The
pipeline would need review and the claim would need revision.
Claim 2 — Bounded execution (WCET)
Statement. No input causes the pipeline to consume more than WCET_CYCLES = 800
cycles.
Test. Run 1,000,000 inputs (in-domain and adversarial). Read the hardware cycle counter at
entry and exit of every query. Record the maximum.
Fail condition. Any input that exceeds 800 cycles. Either the budget is wrong or the algorithm
is not bounded. Both are findings worth publishing.
Claim 3 — Fail-closed
Statement. Any input that does not uniquely match a known command returns ERR_AMBIG.
No out-of-domain input ever returns a valid tile.
Test. Feed 50,000 adversarial inputs across five categories:
· Out-of-domain English (Project Gutenberg snippets)
· Random byte strings
· Malformed commands (stop line, stop line 99, unicode garbage)
· Near-misses within edit distance 1 of two tiles at once
· Adversarial payloads (stop line 1; delete all)
Fail condition. Any adversarial input that returns a valid tile. That would mean the triage
accepts ambiguity, which the whole safety claim rests on it refusing.
What we are not testing
· Energy per query (requires an accurate shunt + scope; optional, sketched in §7)
· Ternary arithmetic (the matcher is string-based; ternary comes next)
· The tiny transformer (substituted by a lookup table for this artifact)
· SK-1024, 1024 tiles, ROM/SRAM split, four-norm certification
These are the next artifacts. This one proves the shape.
