§7 — Expected results
Host build (baseline — you will see these)
```
$ ./st1024 --bench /tmp/inputs.txt
Queries: 1000000
Max: 4200 ns
Mean: 380 ns
Budget: 800
Pass: NO
```
That "Pass: NO" on the host is expected and honest. The host clock_gettime is not a cycle
counter. It is a nanosecond wall clock with all the scheduling noise of a general-purpose OS.
It cannot resolve an 800-cycle budget. This is why you do the MCU build.
MCU build (the numbers that matter)
On a Cortex-M7 at 480 MHz with DWT->CYCCNT:
Metric Expected Pass condition
Max cycles per query 600–900 ≤ 800
Mean cycles per query 300–500 —
Determinism (1M × 2 runs) identical SHA-256 exact match
Fail-closed (50k adversarial) 100% ERR_AMBIG 100%
If max cycles exceeds 800, that is a finding, not a failure. Either the budget is wrong or the
algorithm is not bounded. Both are worth publishing. Do not fudge the number.
If fail-closed is anything other than 100%, that is a bug and it needs to be fixed before
publishing. The safety claim rests on this.
Energy (optional — only if you have a Joulescope or PPK2)
Metric Plausible range
Energy per query 5–50 μJ
Energy per idle period < 1 μW
Comparison (TinyLlama on laptop) 500,000–2,000,000 μJ per classification
The comparison is not a fair benchmark (different hardware, different task). It is an
order-of-magnitude sanity check. If your MCU number comes in under 100 μJ, you have
shown the shape of the claim.
