The repo is the artifact. Everything else is a pointer to it.
2. Zenodo — the DOI
Once the GitHub repo is stable, publish a snapshot to Zenodo. You get a DOI. The DOI
makes the artifact citable in papers, standards submissions, and procurement documents.
This matters more than it sounds. A GitHub commit is a record. A DOI is a record that
survives you deleting your GitHub account. Industrial adoption requires the second one.
